---

# Surreal.js Skill

Surreal: ~320-line, no-dependency, no-build-step JS library.
Core idea — **Locality of Behavior (LoB)**: behavior lives next to HTML element via inline `<script>` tags.

CDN: `<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/gnat/surreal@main/surreal.js"></script>`
Download: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/gnat/surreal/main/surreal.js`
Pinned commit: `ffc4525`

---

## Core Concepts

### `me()` vs `any()`

| Function | Returns | Use when… |
|----------|---------|-----------|
| `me()` | 1 element (or null) | targeting a single element |
| `any()` | array (or `[]`) | targeting multiple elements |

Solves classic jQuery `$()` ambiguity.

### Locality of Behavior — the key pattern

```html
<div class="card">
  <script>
    me().on("click", ev => { me(ev).classToggle("active") })
  </script>
</div>
```

`me()` with **no arguments** inside inline `<script>` returns **parent element** — no id/class needed.

---

## Selectors

```js
me()              // parent element of the inline <script>
me("body")        // <body>
me(".btn")        // first .btn
me("#header")     // #header
me(event)         // element that fired the event (uses currentTarget)
me('-')           // previous sibling (for void elements like <input>)
me('prev')        // same as me('-')

any(".btn")       // ALL .btn elements → array
any("button", me("#nav"))  // buttons *inside* #nav only
```

---

## Full API Reference

### DOM Manipulation (chainable off `me()` / `any()`)

```js
// Class
me().classAdd('active')        // leading . optional: classAdd('.active')
me().classRemove('active')
me().classToggle('active')
// Aliases: class_add / addClass / add_class (snake_case or camelCase both work)

// Styles
me().styles('color: red')
me().styles({ color: 'red', background: 'blue' })
me().styles({ background: null })   // remove a style

// Attributes
me().attribute('data-x')                          // get
me().attribute('data-x', true)                    // set
me().attribute({ 'data-x': 'yes', 'data-y': 'no' }) // set multiple
me().attribute('data-x', null)                    // remove
// Aliases: attributes / attr

// Events
me().on('click', ev => { ... })     // addEventListener wrapper
me().off('click', fn)               // removeEventListener wrapper
me().offAll()                       // remove all listeners
me().disable()                      // disable click/key/submit events
me().enable()                       // re-enable

// Custom events
me().send('change')
me().send('change', { data: 'thing' })   // with detail payload
// Alias: trigger

// Iteration
me().run(e => { console.log(e) })   // forEach but also works on single elements
any('.btn').run(e => { ... })

// Remove element
me().remove()
```

### Global Utility Functions

```js
sleep(ms)              // async setTimeout — use with await for timelines
await sleep(1000)

tick()                 // await one animation frame (requestAnimationFrame)
await tick()

rAF(fn)                // requestAnimationFrame alias
rIC(fn)                // requestIdleCallback alias

halt(event)            // stopPropagation + preventDefault

createElement("div")   // document.createElement alias
create_element("div")  // snake_case alias

onloadAdd(fn)          // safe window.onload — stacks, doesn't overwrite
```

### Built-in Effects (Plugins)

```js
me().fadeOut()                              // fade out and remove
me().fadeOut(callback, 3000)               // over 3s then call fn
me().fadeIn()                              // fade in (element must have opacity:0)
me().fadeIn(callback, 3000)
// Aliases: fade_out / fade_in
```

---

## Common Patterns

### Inline event handler (most common)
```html
<button>
  Click me
  <script>me().on("click", ev => { alert("hi!") })</script>
</button>
```

### Async animation timeline
```html
<div class="box">
  <script>
    me().on("click", async ev => {
      let el = me(ev)           // save ref — async loses `this`
      me(el).styles({ transition: "background 1s" })
      await sleep(1000); me(el).styles({ background: "red" })
      await sleep(1000); me(el).styles({ background: "blue" })
      await sleep(1000); me(el).fadeOut()
    })
  </script>
</div>
```

### Run immediately on load
```html
<div>
  <script>
    (async (el = me()) => {
      await sleep(500)
      me(el).classAdd("visible")
    })()
  </script>
</div>
```

### Select void element (`<input>`, `<hr>`, etc.)
```html
<input type="text" />
<script>me('-').value = "hello"</script>
```

### Scope variables inside inline scripts
```html
<script>
  {   // block scope with { }
    let msg = "Hello"
    function greet(t) { alert(t) }
    me().on("click", () => greet(msg))
  }
</script>
```

### Attach methods directly to element
```html
<script>
  me().greet = (text) => { alert(text) }
  me().on("click", ev => { me(ev).greet("hi") })
</script>
```

### Guard against missing elements
```js
me("#might-not-exist")?.classAdd("active")
me("#might-not-exist", document, false)?.classAdd("active")  // suppress warn
```

### onloadAdd — safe deferred execution
```html
<script>
  let e = me()
  onloadAdd(_ => { me(e).on("click", ev => { alert("loaded") }) })
</script>
```

---

## Pairing with htmx

htmx handles AJAX; surreal handles local behavior:

```html
<!-- htmx handles the AJAX; surreal handles the local behavior -->
<button hx-post="/do-thing" hx-swap="outerHTML">
  Do thing
  <script>
    me().on("htmx:afterRequest", ev => {
      me(ev).classAdd("done")
    })
  </script>
</button>
```

---

## Using Globals vs Namespaced

`globalsAdd()` exposes everything (`me`, `any`, `sleep`, etc.) globally by default.
Remove `globalsAdd()` for fully namespaced:

```js
surreal.me().classAdd('red')
surreal.classAdd(surreal.me(), 'red')
```

---

## Writing a Plugin

```js
function pluginHighlight(e) {
  e.highlight = (color = "yellow") => {
    e.styles({ background: color })
    return e   // always return e for chainability
  }
}
surreal.plugins.push(pluginHighlight)

// Now usable everywhere:
me().highlight("pink")
```

---

## Things Surreal Does NOT Need to Handle (Use Vanilla JS)

```js
// Text content
me().textContent = "safe text"
me().innerHTML   = "<p>html</p>"

// Children
me().children
me().prepend(newEl)
me().appendChild(newEl)

// AJAX → use fetch() or htmx
me().on("click", async ev => {
  let res = await fetch("/endpoint")
  if (res.ok) me(ev).innerHTML = await res.text()
})

// Logging
console.log(), console.warn()
monitorEvents(me())   // Chrome DevTools event monitor
```

---

## Key Reminders

1. `me()` = **single element** (parent `<script>` by default)
2. `any()` = **array** of elements
3. In `async` handlers, **save `me(ev)` before first `await`** — event target lost after async gaps.
4. `sleep()` needs `await` inside `async` function.
5. `type="module"` loses parent-element scoping — use explicit selector with `me()`.
6. Naming flexible: `classAdd` = `class_add` = `addClass` = `add_class`.

</surreal-js>
